1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,647
Hello again, hackers. Previously we've gone on

2
00:00:02,447 --> 00:00:05,222
a journey to explore how data overflows

3
00:00:05,199 --> 00:00:08,449
and how print functions leak useful data.

4
00:00:08,298 --> 00:00:10,912
It's time to get crafty with our exploits.

5
00:00:10,824 --> 00:00:12,770
With the same idea as before,

6
00:00:12,570 --> 00:00:15,306
data and text, ultimately numbers and electric

7
00:00:15,274 --> 00:00:18,326
cells inside tiny transistors, we turn our

8
00:00:18,126 --> 00:00:20,418
text into something more dangerous than the

9
00:00:20,267 --> 00:00:22,657
addresses and filler bytes last time.

10
00:00:22,457 --> 00:00:24,591
This time, our text turns into real

11
00:00:24,391 --> 00:00:27,196
commands for the targeted program.

12
00:00:27,379 --> 00:00:29,900
But before we touch the dangerous idea,

13
00:00:29,759 --> 00:00:32,719
freeze the room. This is a glass-box lab.

14
00:00:32,519 --> 00:00:34,059
Nothing here is a real target,

15
00:00:33,859 --> 00:00:35,819
nothing here is a real payload.

16
00:00:35,619 --> 00:00:37,409
We are going to watch the concept

17
00:00:37,209 --> 00:00:40,159
like a machine behind thick museum glass.

18
00:00:42,548 --> 00:00:44,638
Because the magic trick is not that

19
00:00:44,588 --> 00:00:46,878
text is special. The magic trick is

20
00:00:46,758 --> 00:00:49,018
that the machine does not care what

21
00:00:48,888 --> 00:00:51,428
humans call text. To the machine,

22
00:00:51,228 --> 00:00:54,468
text is just bytes. Bytes are just numbers.

23
00:00:54,268 --> 00:00:56,958
Numbers are just patterns of voltage.

24
00:00:57,777 --> 00:01:00,017
And instructions are also bytes.

25
00:00:59,817 --> 00:01:02,307
A command is not written in lightning.

26
00:01:02,107 --> 00:01:03,977
It is not born with a crown.

27
00:01:03,847 --> 00:01:05,817
It is just a byte pattern the

28
00:01:05,997 --> 00:01:09,957
processor decides to read as an action.

29
00:01:10,127 --> 00:01:13,819
So the whole story becomes a question of attention.

30
00:01:13,619 --> 00:01:16,426
Where is the program looking right now?

31
00:01:16,354 --> 00:01:19,205
Which byte is the next byte it believes?

32
00:01:19,223 --> 00:01:21,704
Normally, your input sits politely in the

33
00:01:21,572 --> 00:01:23,387
data section of the story.

34
00:01:23,246 --> 00:01:25,521
It is printed, compared, copied,

35
00:01:25,419 --> 00:01:27,909
maybe rejected. It is not supposed to

36
00:01:27,709 --> 00:01:28,995
become the narrator.

37
00:01:29,432 --> 00:01:31,877
But with an overflow, we saw the

38
00:01:31,677 --> 00:01:34,280
basket does not always stay a basket.

39
00:01:34,179 --> 00:01:36,772
Too much input presses against the walls,

40
00:01:36,730 --> 00:01:40,609
the walls bend, then nearby memory starts getting touched.

41
00:01:40,652 --> 00:01:42,712
Last time, that was enough to leak

42
00:01:42,622 --> 00:01:45,092
secrets and move numbers around.

43
00:01:44,952 --> 00:01:47,012
This time, the important thing is what

44
00:01:46,812 --> 00:01:48,532
sits next to the input:

45
00:01:48,432 --> 00:01:50,462
not just storage, but the map of

46
00:01:50,372 --> 00:01:52,282
where execution goes next.

47
00:01:53,779 --> 00:01:56,009
Imagine the program as a train.

48
00:01:55,809 --> 00:01:57,989
Every instruction is a station.

49
00:01:57,919 --> 00:01:59,939
The return address is the ticket that

50
00:01:59,769 --> 00:02:02,199
says where the train goes after this

51
00:02:02,119 --> 00:02:03,369
function is done.

52
00:02:05,709 --> 00:02:07,269
A normal return is boring,

53
00:02:07,279 --> 00:02:11,419
and boring is beautiful. The function finishes,

54
00:02:11,219 --> 00:02:13,349
reads its saved ticket, and goes home.

55
00:02:17,428 --> 00:02:19,778
But if an overflow can smudge the ticket,

56
00:02:19,628 --> 00:02:21,498
the train may not go home.

57
00:02:21,348 --> 00:02:22,848
It may jump to a place the

58
00:02:22,648 --> 00:02:25,338
original programmer never planned to visit.

59
00:02:30,395 --> 00:02:32,115
This is the core idea.

60
00:02:31,995 --> 00:02:34,675
We are not making magic commands from nothing.

61
00:02:34,555 --> 00:02:36,355
We are arranging bytes so that the

62
00:02:36,155 --> 00:02:38,835
program's own attention lands somewhere dangerous.

63
00:02:43,313 --> 00:02:46,123
In real machines, protections exist to make

64
00:02:46,013 --> 00:02:48,443
that jump fail. So in our animation,

65
00:02:48,283 --> 00:02:50,532
every dangerous surface gets a guard:

66
00:02:50,433 --> 00:02:53,363
permissions, randomness, seals, and rules about where

67
00:02:53,363 --> 00:02:54,853
execution may travel.

68
00:02:56,263 --> 00:02:59,673
For this episode, we stay before the bypasses.

69
00:02:59,473 --> 00:03:01,603
We are learning the shape of the monster,

70
00:03:01,403 --> 00:03:02,413
not feeding it.

71
00:03:08,018 --> 00:03:10,603
Now zoom in. The processor lives by

72
00:03:10,538 --> 00:03:13,152
a tiny rhythm: fetch, decode,

73
00:03:13,000 --> 00:03:14,998
execute. Fetch the next bytes.

74
00:03:14,798 --> 00:03:17,662
Decode what they mean. Execute the action.

75
00:03:17,462 --> 00:03:20,962
Then repeat, millions and billions of times.

76
00:03:20,962 --> 00:03:25,121
This loop is so fast it feels like personality.

77
00:03:24,972 --> 00:03:27,922
But underneath, it is a machine asking

78
00:03:27,722 --> 00:03:31,481
the same question forever: what is next?

79
00:03:31,281 --> 00:03:33,561
What is next? What is next?

80
00:03:34,012 --> 00:03:36,200
A command, then, is just a byte

81
00:03:36,042 --> 00:03:39,297
wearing the instruction costume while the spotlight

82
00:03:39,202 --> 00:03:40,041
is on it.

83
00:03:47,459 --> 00:03:50,109
That is why exploit animation should not

84
00:03:49,909 --> 00:03:52,609
show evil text turning into evil code

85
00:03:52,419 --> 00:03:56,149
like a curse. It should show interpretation changing.

86
00:03:56,019 --> 00:03:57,979
Same material, different role.

87
00:04:01,240 --> 00:04:02,990
Now let's build the toy stage.

88
00:04:03,210 --> 00:04:05,660
On the left, input arrives as a river.

89
00:04:05,780 --> 00:04:08,580
In the middle, a small function copies

90
00:04:08,380 --> 00:04:10,420
it into a cup. On the right,

91
00:04:10,220 --> 00:04:12,810
the return path waits like a little bridge.

92
00:04:15,230 --> 00:04:17,400
As long as the river is measured,

93
00:04:17,200 --> 00:04:20,710
everything is peaceful. The cup fills to the line,

94
00:04:20,650 --> 00:04:22,260
the function uses the data,

95
00:04:22,060 --> 00:04:23,670
and the bridge remains dry.

96
00:04:29,071 --> 00:04:31,671
But if the river ignores the cup,

97
00:04:31,471 --> 00:04:35,201
pressure becomes movement. Movement becomes corruption.

98
00:04:35,071 --> 00:04:37,231
Corruption becomes a new question:

99
00:04:37,191 --> 00:04:39,121
did we only break the program,

100
00:04:38,991 --> 00:04:40,281
or did we steer it?

101
00:04:42,970 --> 00:04:45,890
Most bad input just crashes things.

102
00:04:45,690 --> 00:04:49,130
That matters. A crash is not boring.

103
00:04:48,980 --> 00:04:50,880
A crash is the machine telling you

104
00:04:50,760 --> 00:04:52,800
something touched a part of the story

105
00:04:52,690 --> 00:04:53,850
it should not touch.

106
00:04:56,970 --> 00:05:00,850
Controlled behavior is different. Controlled behavior means

107
00:05:00,790 --> 00:05:03,100
the bad input does not merely knock

108
00:05:02,900 --> 00:05:05,290
the train over. It changes the switch

109
00:05:05,200 --> 00:05:07,050
while the train is still moving.

110
00:05:11,794 --> 00:05:14,594
This is where our visual language matters.

111
00:05:14,494 --> 00:05:16,584
We do not need real bytes to

112
00:05:16,384 --> 00:05:19,794
explain real danger. We need a clear model:

113
00:05:19,594 --> 00:05:24,494
pressure reaches control data, control data changes attention,

114
00:05:24,294 --> 00:05:26,094
attention reads different bytes.

115
00:05:26,894 --> 00:05:29,914
Say it again smaller. Too much text enters.

116
00:05:29,754 --> 00:05:32,804
Nearby memory changes. The program looks somewhere new.

117
00:05:32,604 --> 00:05:35,294
Bytes at the new place get interpreted as actions.

118
00:05:42,403 --> 00:05:44,893
And smaller again. Input becomes shape.

119
00:05:44,803 --> 00:05:47,643
Shape changes direction. Direction changes meaning.

120
00:05:57,570 --> 00:06:00,230
The phrase code execution sounds like the

121
00:06:00,030 --> 00:06:02,210
machine is possessed. But really,

122
00:06:02,010 --> 00:06:05,090
it means the control-flow spotlight reached bytes

123
00:06:04,900 --> 00:06:07,780
that were not supposed to be the next instructions.

124
00:06:13,137 --> 00:06:15,577
A real processor does not ask,

125
00:06:15,417 --> 00:06:17,137
did a human type this?

126
00:06:16,977 --> 00:06:19,577
It asks, am I allowed to execute here,

127
00:06:19,417 --> 00:06:27,535
and what do these bytes decode to?

128
00:06:28,855 --> 00:06:31,927
Modern systems try hard to answer no.

129
00:06:31,805 --> 00:06:34,015
Data pages often say, store here,

130
00:06:33,985 --> 00:06:35,395
but do not execute here.

131
00:06:45,031 --> 00:06:47,231
That is the no-execute idea.

132
00:06:47,091 --> 00:06:49,131
Even if bytes land in memory,

133
00:06:48,991 --> 00:06:52,051
the floor itself refuses to become a stage.

134
00:07:01,287 --> 00:07:03,947
Another defense is randomness. If an attacker

135
00:07:03,747 --> 00:07:05,426
needs to guess where something lives,

136
00:07:05,317 --> 00:07:08,147
the operating system can rearrange the furniture

137
00:07:08,077 --> 00:07:09,967
every time the program starts.

138
00:07:17,849 --> 00:07:21,160
From the outside, the room becomes a shifting maze.

139
00:07:21,049 --> 00:07:23,610
The concept is simple: if you cannot

140
00:07:23,409 --> 00:07:26,910
trust fixed locations, steering gets harder.

141
00:07:34,194 --> 00:07:36,444
Stack canaries add a fragile seal near

142
00:07:36,244 --> 00:07:40,244
important control data. Overflow touches the seal,

143
00:07:40,144 --> 00:07:42,854
the program notices, and the trip ends

144
00:07:42,654 --> 00:07:45,114
before the return ticket gets used.

145
00:07:51,698 --> 00:07:54,238
Control-flow rules go one step higher.

146
00:07:54,038 --> 00:07:56,068
They ask whether the next jump makes

147
00:07:55,898 --> 00:08:07,130
sense compared with the program's original map.

148
00:08:08,957 --> 00:08:12,337
So when people say exploit development is a fight,

149
00:08:12,137 --> 00:08:14,257
this is the fight: one side tries

150
00:08:14,057 --> 00:08:17,007
to change attention, the other side builds

151
00:08:16,927 --> 00:08:19,227
rules so attention cannot be fooled.

152
00:08:25,606 --> 00:08:28,396
Let's return to our toy command bytes.

153
00:08:28,246 --> 00:08:29,836
We will make them harmless.

154
00:08:29,726 --> 00:08:32,046
No shell, no system calls,

155
00:08:31,946 --> 00:08:34,976
no real machine instructions. Just colored cards

156
00:08:34,776 --> 00:08:37,366
that tell a toy robot to blink,

157
00:08:37,166 --> 00:08:38,716
draw a square, and stop.

158
00:08:42,631 --> 00:08:44,901
The safety of the toy matters because

159
00:08:44,701 --> 00:08:46,381
the shape is the lesson.

160
00:08:46,181 --> 00:08:49,091
The content is not. Dangerous payload details

161
00:08:48,891 --> 00:08:51,241
would distract from the machine truth.

162
00:08:59,083 --> 00:09:02,193
Now place those toy cards in the input area.

163
00:09:01,993 --> 00:09:05,413
Nothing happens yet. They are still data.

164
00:09:05,283 --> 00:09:08,013
They sit there like actors waiting backstage.

165
00:09:15,487 --> 00:09:17,867
Then imagine the return ticket gets changed

166
00:09:17,777 --> 00:09:20,587
in our symbolic model. Not to a real address.

167
00:09:20,407 --> 00:09:21,558
Not to a real location.

168
00:09:21,437 --> 00:09:23,567
Just to the idea of backstage.

169
00:09:32,088 --> 00:09:34,449
The train follows the changed ticket.

170
00:09:34,348 --> 00:09:37,038
The spotlight moves. The sleeping cards wake

171
00:09:36,918 --> 00:09:39,818
up because the processor is now treating

172
00:09:39,628 --> 00:09:42,078
that area as the next thing to decode.

173
00:09:48,708 --> 00:09:53,028
Same card. Different role. One moment it is text.

174
00:09:52,838 --> 00:09:55,104
The next moment, under the wrong spotlight,

175
00:09:55,088 --> 00:09:56,348
it becomes an action.

176
00:10:05,043 --> 00:10:06,943
This is the sentence the whole episode

177
00:10:06,853 --> 00:10:09,583
is built around: data does not execute

178
00:10:09,553 --> 00:10:12,043
because it is evil. Data executes when

179
00:10:11,843 --> 00:10:15,543
control flow is tricked into treating it as instructions,

180
00:10:15,363 --> 00:10:17,633
and defenses fail to stop it.

181
00:10:22,952 --> 00:10:25,431
Now animate the vulnerable moment slowly.

182
00:10:25,362 --> 00:10:27,841
Slower than real life. Absurdly slow.

183
00:10:27,642 --> 00:10:29,731
A single byte crosses a boundary it

184
00:10:29,532 --> 00:10:32,001
was never meant to cross.

185
00:10:40,268 --> 00:10:42,908
Another byte follows. Then another.

186
00:10:42,708 --> 00:10:45,218
They are not smart. They are not angry.

187
00:10:45,098 --> 00:10:47,588
They are just following the copy operation

188
00:10:47,388 --> 00:10:48,828
that failed to stop.

189
00:10:57,764 --> 00:11:00,004
This is why bugs are mechanical.

190
00:10:59,804 --> 00:11:02,933
A vulnerability is often not a dramatic villain.

191
00:11:02,884 --> 00:11:05,304
It is a tiny missing rule repeated

192
00:11:05,104 --> 00:11:07,174
at machine speed.

193
00:11:15,040 --> 00:11:17,680
Then the important drawer gets touched.

194
00:11:17,570 --> 00:11:21,040
In our toy model, the return ticket ink changes.

195
00:11:21,010 --> 00:11:23,660
The next destination is no longer the normal station.

196
00:11:32,436 --> 00:11:35,866
The function finishes. That part is important.

197
00:11:35,686 --> 00:11:38,202
The program does not instantly teleport.

198
00:11:38,146 --> 00:11:40,336
It keeps doing normal work until the

199
00:11:40,136 --> 00:11:42,586
moment it needs the saved return path.

200
00:11:49,941 --> 00:11:53,451
Then, at return, the corrupted direction matters.

201
00:11:53,301 --> 00:11:55,331
The train asks where to go,

202
00:11:55,131 --> 00:11:58,051
reads the changed ticket, and the switch moves.

203
00:12:07,289 --> 00:12:09,519
The spotlight arrives at the input.

204
00:12:09,459 --> 00:12:12,149
The card wakes. Decode begins.

205
00:12:11,969 --> 00:12:22,337
The toy robot blinks.

206
00:12:24,251 --> 00:12:26,951
Next card. Draw square. Next card.

207
00:12:26,921 --> 00:12:29,431
Stop. The processor does not know this

208
00:12:29,231 --> 00:12:30,691
was typed by a human.

209
00:12:30,671 --> 00:12:33,121
It only follows the path it was given.

210
00:12:41,696 --> 00:12:43,436
That is the clean mental model.

211
00:12:43,276 --> 00:12:45,656
Overflow does not equal execution.

212
00:12:45,556 --> 00:12:48,436
Leaking an address does not equal execution.

213
00:12:48,276 --> 00:12:50,596
Crashing does not equal execution.

214
00:12:50,436 --> 00:12:53,116
Execution needs control flow to land on

215
00:12:52,916 --> 00:12:55,956
decodable bytes in an executable place.

216
00:12:59,614 --> 00:13:01,854
Every gate can fail closed.

217
00:13:01,654 --> 00:13:03,614
The address can be unknown.

218
00:13:03,474 --> 00:13:05,774
The region can refuse execution.

219
00:13:05,834 --> 00:13:08,434
The seal can break and stop the program.

220
00:13:08,244 --> 00:13:11,254
The control-flow map can reject the path.

221
00:13:18,090 --> 00:13:21,510
And in secure software, we want boring endings.

222
00:13:21,430 --> 00:13:24,150
The safest exploit animation is the one

223
00:13:23,950 --> 00:13:25,920
where the train hits a guardrail and

224
00:13:25,820 --> 00:13:27,510
the program says no.

225
00:13:36,468 --> 00:13:40,417
So how do programmers make the ending boring?

226
00:13:40,478 --> 00:13:44,428
They measure input. They use safe copying patterns.

227
00:13:44,258 --> 00:13:46,648
They separate code from data.

228
00:13:46,478 --> 00:13:50,908
They let compiler and operating-system defenses stay turned on.

229
00:13:55,014 --> 00:13:57,354
Measuring input is the first fix because

230
00:13:57,224 --> 00:13:59,564
it attacks the bug at the source.

231
00:13:59,414 --> 00:14:01,264
The river should never be allowed to

232
00:14:01,064 --> 00:14:02,644
pretend the cup is endless.

233
00:14:13,472 --> 00:14:16,202
Separating code and data is the second

234
00:14:16,002 --> 00:14:18,542
fix because it attacks interpretation.

235
00:14:18,472 --> 00:14:21,812
Even if data exists, the stage refuses

236
00:14:21,632 --> 00:14:23,492
to perform it.

237
00:14:31,603 --> 00:14:35,363
Randomness attacks prediction. The room moves.

238
00:14:35,243 --> 00:14:37,923
The furniture moves. The attacker's map gets

239
00:14:37,723 --> 00:14:40,003
stale the moment the process starts.

240
00:14:49,756 --> 00:14:53,036
Canaries attack silent corruption. They turn invisible

241
00:14:52,836 --> 00:14:55,216
damage into a loud alarm before the

242
00:14:55,016 --> 00:14:56,586
return path is trusted.

243
00:15:07,830 --> 00:15:11,020
Control-flow checks attack weird travel.

244
00:15:10,820 --> 00:15:12,740
They make sure the train only moves

245
00:15:12,670 --> 00:15:15,630
along routes that belonged to the program's design.

246
00:15:26,826 --> 00:15:30,836
Now pull all of this into one living diagram.

247
00:15:30,636 --> 00:15:34,186
On the left, the bug.

248
00:15:34,006 --> 00:15:35,886
In the center, control flow.

249
00:15:35,786 --> 00:15:39,616
On the right, defenses. The episode is

250
00:15:39,616 --> 00:15:43,146
not about memorizing tricks. It is about

251
00:15:43,006 --> 00:15:45,386
seeing the physics of execution.

252
00:15:45,756 --> 00:15:47,946
The reason this feels strange is that

253
00:15:47,746 --> 00:15:51,836
software is abstract, but the consequences are physical.

254
00:15:51,636 --> 00:15:54,156
A bit flips. A switch changes.

255
00:15:53,956 --> 00:15:56,396
A processor steps somewhere else.

256
00:16:04,968 --> 00:16:07,008
If you remember only one image,

257
00:16:06,808 --> 00:16:09,788
remember the spotlight. Bytes are everywhere,

258
00:16:09,588 --> 00:16:12,207
but only the bytes under the spotlight

259
00:16:12,138 --> 00:16:14,148
become the next instruction.

260
00:16:24,142 --> 00:16:27,242
And if you remember only one defense image,

261
00:16:27,042 --> 00:16:29,712
remember the locked floor. Data can sit

262
00:16:29,512 --> 00:16:32,420
there all day, but the floor can say,

263
00:16:32,292 --> 00:16:33,602
no stage here.

264
00:16:43,514 --> 00:16:46,253
Now replay the entire episode in ten seconds,

265
00:16:46,053 --> 00:16:59,467
like a flipbook.

266
00:17:02,235 --> 00:17:04,465
First pass: the attacker's dream.

267
00:17:04,285 --> 00:17:08,055
Input reaches control data, control flow moves,

268
00:17:07,995 --> 00:17:09,575
bytes decode as behavior.

269
00:17:21,034 --> 00:17:23,624
Second pass: the defender's dream.

270
00:17:23,424 --> 00:17:26,224
Input is measured, the ticket stays clean,

271
00:17:26,113 --> 00:17:28,914
data remains data, and the train goes home.

272
00:17:40,089 --> 00:17:42,429
Both stories use the same ingredients:

273
00:17:42,229 --> 00:17:59,183
bytes, memory, attention. The difference is whether boundaries hold.

274
00:17:59,972 --> 00:18:02,642
So when you hear turning text into commands,

275
00:18:02,442 --> 00:18:04,862
translate it in your head.

276
00:18:04,662 --> 00:18:08,362
Not wizardry. Not words becoming alive.

277
00:18:08,222 --> 00:18:10,877
Just bytes landing where the program's next-step

278
00:18:10,812 --> 00:18:13,182
machinery treats them as instructions.

279
00:18:20,264 --> 00:18:21,864
And the safest way to learn it

280
00:18:21,664 --> 00:18:24,144
is with toy commands, toy memory,

281
00:18:24,124 --> 00:18:26,484
and real respect for the boundary between

282
00:18:26,334 --> 00:18:27,864
explanation and weapon.

283
00:18:40,371 --> 00:18:42,811
One more close-up before we leave.

284
00:18:42,661 --> 00:18:44,071
Look at this byte. Alone,

285
00:18:43,871 --> 00:18:46,707
it means nothing. In a text viewer,

286
00:18:46,611 --> 00:18:48,961
it is a character. In a file,

287
00:18:48,871 --> 00:18:51,531
it is data. Under the instruction pointer,

288
00:18:51,331 --> 00:19:00,546
with permission, it becomes a step.

289
00:19:01,952 --> 00:19:04,672
That is the beauty and the horror of computers.

290
00:19:04,502 --> 00:19:07,094
They are consistent. They are obedient.

291
00:19:07,112 --> 00:19:09,262
And if we give them the wrong path,

292
00:19:09,062 --> 00:19:11,222
they obey the wrong path perfectly.

293
00:19:23,248 --> 00:19:26,658
Next time, we can use this model

294
00:19:26,458 --> 00:19:30,448
to talk about why mitigations changed exploit history.

295
00:19:30,248 --> 00:19:31,938
Not as a bag of tricks,

296
00:19:31,738 --> 00:19:35,078
but as architectural guardrails placed exactly where

297
00:19:34,878 --> 00:19:38,138
the chain can break.

298
00:19:44,623 --> 00:19:46,759
For now, keep the chain in your head:

299
00:19:46,663 --> 00:19:50,463
overflow changes memory, memory changes direction,

300
00:19:50,623 --> 00:19:54,893
direction changes interpretation, interpretation becomes behavior.

301
00:20:05,416 --> 00:20:08,696
And keep the defender's version right beside it:

302
00:20:08,526 --> 00:20:11,176
measure the input, protect the return path,

303
00:20:11,006 --> 00:20:13,986
separate data from code, verify the route.

304
00:20:26,427 --> 00:20:28,797
That is how we make the invisible visible.

305
00:20:28,597 --> 00:20:31,217
A stack becomes a shelf.

306
00:20:31,017 --> 00:20:33,737
A return address becomes a ticket.

307
00:20:33,627 --> 00:20:36,577
A processor becomes a spotlight.

308
00:20:36,467 --> 00:20:40,577
And text becoming commands becomes a story about attention.

309
00:20:47,881 --> 00:20:49,671
Thanks for watching. Stay curious,

310
00:20:49,471 --> 00:20:51,661
stay careful, and remember: the machine only

311
00:20:51,461 --> 00:20:53,481
does what the path tells it to do.
